<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch7-section-mechanical-energy-equation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mechanical energy equation</title>

  <p>
    You learned in your physics course
    that a change in the <term>mechanical energy</term> (sum of macroscopic kinetic and potential energies) of a closed system
    is accomplished through work done on the system by non-conservative forces.
    You may have seen this statement of <term>mechanical energy conservation</term> expressed as
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mechanical-energy-conservation-simple" number="yes">
        \Delta K + \Delta U = W_{nc}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where <m>\Delta K = K_2 - K_1</m> [J] is the change in kinetic energy <m>K</m>,
    <m>\Delta U = U_2 - U_1</m> [J] is the change in potential energy <m>U</m>,
    and <m>W_{nc}</m> [J] is work done on the system by non-conservative forces.
  </p>

  <p>
    We can build a similar statement of mechanical energy conservation using the equation of motion.
  </p>

  <p>
    Following the derivation of <xref ref="martin" text="custom">Martin (2006)</xref>,
    we dot the vector equation of motion with <m>\vec{u}</m> to produce the following component equations:
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-x" number="yes">
        \frac{D}{Dt} \left( \frac{u^2}{2} \right)
        +
        \frac{u^2 w}{a}
        -
        \frac{u^2 v \tan{\phi}}{a}
        =
        -\frac{u}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
        +
        fuv
        -
        2 \Omega uw \cos{\phi}
        +
        \nu u \nabla^2 u
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-y" number="yes">
        \frac{D}{Dt} \left( \frac{v^2}{2} \right)
        +
        \frac{v^2 w}{a}
        +
        \frac{u^2 v \tan{\phi}}{a}
        =
        -\frac{v}{\rho}\frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
        -
        fuv
        +
        \nu v \nabla^2 v
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-z" number="yes">
        \frac{D}{Dt} \left( \frac{w^2}{2} \right)
        -
        \frac{w \left(u^2 +v^2 \right)}{a}
        =
        -\frac{w}{\rho}\frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
        -
        gw
        +
        2 \Omega u w \cos{\phi}
        +
        \nu w \nabla^2 w
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where the property <m>\dfrac{d}{dx} \left( \dfrac{y^2}{2} \right) = y \dfrac{dy}{dx}</m>
    has been applied to the first term on the left-hand side of each equation.
  </p>

  <p>
    We add <xref first="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-x" last="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-z" />,
    which leads to cancellation of all products originating from the curvature and Coriolis terms.
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-start" number="yes">
        \frac{D}{Dt} \left(
        \frac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}
        \right)
        =
        -\frac{1}{\rho} \vec{u} \cdot \nabla p
        - gw
        +
        \nu \vec{u} \cdot \nabla^2 \vec{u}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Physically,
    these terms cancel because they do no work on the wind,
    and therefore they do not change its kinetic or potential energies.
  </p>

  <p>
    The term on the left-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-start" text="type-global" />
    represents the time rate of change of the total kinetic energy per unit mass of an air parcel.
    Considering this alongside the statement of mechanical energy conservation <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-conservation-simple" text="type-global" />,
    the terms on the right-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-start" text="type-global" />
    must represent the sum of the negative of the time rate of change of the air parcel's potential energy per unit mass
    and the time rate of change of the non-conservative work per unit mass being done on the air parcel.
  </p>

  <p>
    We proceed by rewriting a term on the right-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-start" text="type-global" />
    to make this connection clearer.
    Since <m>w = \dfrac{Dz}{Dt}</m>,
    by the definition of geopotential,
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>
        gw = g \frac{Dz}{Dt} = \frac{D \Phi}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    and so <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-start" text="type-global" /> can be written
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" number="yes">
        \frac{D}{Dt} \left(
        \frac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}
        + \Phi
        \right)
        =
        -\frac{1}{\rho} \vec{u} \cdot \nabla p
        +
        \nu \vec{u} \cdot \nabla^2 \vec{u}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
    is the <term>mechanical energy equation</term>.
    It is not a "new" or "separate" equation: It is a combination and restructuring of the equations of motion.
    Even so,
    it reveals what can cause
    the mechanical energy per unit mass of an air parcel<mdash />which is the sum of its
    kinetic energy per unit mass <m>\dfrac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}</m>
    and its gravity potential energy per unit mass <m>\Phi</m><mdash />to change.
  </p>

  <p>
    Note the similarities between <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
    and <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-conservation-simple" text="type-global" />.
    The left-hand side of each equation gives the change in the macroscopic kinetic and potential energies of the air parcel
    and the right-hand side gives the sum of the work done by non-conservative forces on the air parcel.
    (Keep in mind that these quantities are <term>intensive</term> (per unit mass) in <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
    but are <term>extensive</term> (dependent on mass) in <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-conservation-simple" text="type-global" />.)
  </p>

  <p>
    <xref ref="martin" text="custom">Martin (2006)</xref>
    refers to the first term on the right-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
    as <term>pressure work</term>.
    It represents the work (per unit mass) done by the pressure gradient force on the air parcel,
    and it is proportional to pressure advection <m>-\vec{u} \cdot \nabla p</m>.
    Consequently,
    it is positive (negative) for flow directed from higher pressure toward lower pressure (lower pressure toward higher pressure).
    In other words,
    it is positive (negative) for positive (negative) pressure advection.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second term on the right-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
    represents the dissipation of energy by viscous friction.
  </p>

  <p>
    See <xref ref="holton-hakim" text="custom">Holton and Hakim (2013)</xref>
    for an alternative derivation of <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
    with viscous friction effects neglected.
  </p>

</section>